-- lib/world.cub — the drivers' signature W (PLAN.md revision 7.9, 6 Nov 2026, git 2ca8d747aaa5:PLAN.md, §18.11 “D24–D27 — The world”, its part D26): the commands the machine's driver loop
-- (src/drivers.c, #run) performs against the operating system, and a process as a tree of
-- steps the loop walks. Loaded after lib/proc.cub (Sig, Proc, Cmd, Resp) and lib/prim.cub
-- (Bytes). A process over any signature is run through a handler into W.
--
-- Commands: the time now (nanoseconds), a write of n bytes to a descriptor (the count
-- written), a read of up to n bytes (the count read and the bytes, the buffer n long), a
-- sleep, an exit. Memory commands (load, store, cas, fence on Regions) are lib/proc.cub's
-- MemS, run through sc-world or atomically; a hardware memory model is not offered yet.
data WCmd = w-now | w-write (fd : Word) (n : Nat) (b : Bytes n) | w-read (fd : Word) (n : Nat) | w-sleep (ns : Word) | w-exit (ec : Word)
  | w-open (n : Nat) (path : Bytes n) (flags : Word) (mode : Word) | w-close (fd : Word)
  -- the memory interface (PLAN.md revision 7.9, 6 Nov 2026, git 2ca8d747aaa5:PLAN.md, §18.11 “D24–D27 — The world”, its part D26): words at addresses (ptr-of's, or memory the program does not own),
  -- the model named: load acquire, store release, cas acquire-release (the value seen), fence
  -- sequentially consistent
  | w-load (a : Word) | w-store (a : Word) (v : Word) | w-cas (a : Word) (old : Word) (new : Word) | w-fence
  -- the raw system call (Phase 3; lib/os/linux.cub is the typed world over it): the number and six
  -- arguments as the syscall instruction takes them (rax; rdi rsi rdx r10 r8 r9), the response what
  -- it leaves in rax as a 64-bit word (an error is the two's complement of its errno). Buffers are
  -- addresses: w-alloc n is the address of n fresh zero bytes that stay valid for the process's
  -- life; w-load8/w-store8 are one byte at an address (the value's low 8 bits stored). New
  -- commands are added here, at the end: lib/elf.cub dispatches on the constructors' indices
  | w-sys (nr : Word) (a1 : Word) (a2 : Word) (a3 : Word) (a4 : Word) (a5 : Word) (a6 : Word)
  | w-alloc (n : Word) | w-load8 (a : Word) | w-store8 (a : Word) (v : Word)
  -- another program started (2026-11-16): argv and envp the addresses of NULL-ended arrays of
  -- NUL-ended strings, the program the first of argv; its standard output and error the descriptor
  -- out (a negative out: this process's). The response is its process id, or an errno's two's
  -- complement. Started without copying this process (posix_spawn), so a machine holding much memory
  -- starts one as readily as a small one
  | w-spawn (argv : Word) (envp : Word) (out : Word)
  -- a file read whole as text (the address of its NUL-ended path): 0 and its bytes, or an errno and
  -- nothing; text written whole to a file (created or emptied): the count written, or an errno's
  -- two's complement. Text crosses as one value, not a command per byte
  | w-file-read (path : Word) | w-file-write (path : Word) (t : List Word)
  -- text written whole to a descriptor (a socket, standard output): the count written
  | w-fd-write (fd : Word) (t : List Word)
  -- the same two files, the path given as its name rather than as an address (2026-11-18): the
  -- driver copies the name's bytes and terminates them itself, so no caller holds a pointer and the
  -- driver has no precondition a type does not carry
  | w-file-read-named (path : List Word) | w-file-write-named (path : List Word) (t : List Word)
